## 2. The cause, not the symptom — what this cluster was really cleaning up

**Every one of the four findings is the same defect class wearing four costumes: an assertion that
observes a WEAKER thing than the property it was written for.**

| Finding | The property | What was actually asserted | Cost of the gap |
|---|---|---|---|
| N1 (`t_31623705`) | focus returns to the input from **all three** entry points | focus after the **square** route, four times over | mutant R9 green 14/14 |
| N2 (`t_c0fd0601`) | the value **tracker** is re-synced | the **DOM** value the same line writes | mutant M11 green 14/14 |
| N6 (`t_086c1d78`) | one **user** click runs the handler once | one `.click()`-**method** call, which the platform de-duplicates for you | mutant R1 green 14/14 |
| N1r2 (`t_e7801ab7`) | the **ratio** is below 4.5 | the ratio's **2-dp rendering** parsed back | 424/200,000 alphas misreported |

Three of the four are "the test drove the system through an idiom that is not the user's idiom".
`.click()` sets the HTML spec's click-in-progress flag; a real click does not. `field(x).checked = y`
announces nothing to React; a real click does. The `toFixed(2)` string is what a human reads; the
float is what the threshold means.

**UPGRADE 1 — put the idiom's BLIND SPOT in the plan, next to the idiom.** `SPEC.md` R17's hook
already does the hard half: it names ONE test idiom (`field(name).click()` inside `act`) and lists
the forms measured not to work. Two rounds of review were spent getting that right and it worked —
no seat re-derived it. What it does not carry is the other half: **what that idiom cannot see.**
`.click()` cannot observe anything that depends on the click-in-progress flag, and nothing said so
until a reviewer spent a probe finding it. A plan that names a mandatory idiom owes a
"blind to:" line beside it, and every constant whose wording quantifies over N routes owes a
"pinned for:" line naming which routes a case actually exercises. Both are one line; both would
have removed a whole review round each.

**UPGRADE 2 — the refutation duty needs a MATRIX, not a list.** `heartbeat-worker` §2 asks for
"one neighbouring mutant it should NOT catch" per assertion. Run as a **matrix** — every mutant
against every new pin — it costs the same three runs and produces strictly more:

```
             new pin: focus-return   tracker   re-entrancy
mutant R9  :          RED (x2)       green     green
mutant M11 :          green          RED       green
mutant R1  :          green          green     RED
```

The off-diagonal IS the neighbouring-mutant evidence, and the diagonal being the only RED is the
proof that no pin is over-broad. Three runs, one table, nothing left to argue about. **This should
be in the worker contract as the default shape of §2, not as an option I happened to pick.**

