## 3. What repeatedly cost tokens

**3.1 The zsh word-splitting trap, live, on my first multi-file run — COMMON §10.40 exists and I
still paid it.** `FILES=$(find …); pnpm exec vitest run $FILES` collapsed 14 paths into ONE filter
and printed `No test files found, exiting with code 1`. Cost: one run, ~2 min.
The rule is written; what is missing is that **every multi-file command in a packet should be
delivered as a `.sh` the seat runs, not as a shell line the seat retypes.** The packet already
mandates a `/bin/bash` arm for §10.16 — make the bash arm the PRIMARY artefact and the inline arm
the second opinion, and this class dies. I now do every multi-file run through a `.sh` and never
inline first.

**3.2 The promoted probe-runner's alias list.** Appended to `TOOLING-TRAPS.md` (2026-09-07 04:25).
`:2306` gives the right remedy for running a promoted probe — a scratchpad `--config` whose
`include` names the probe — but the template's `resolve.alias` only covers the probe's own relative
specifiers plus `react`. `SignUpFlow.tsx` imports `@/components/AuthShell`, so the run died at
transform time and printed `Tests  no tests`, which reads exactly like the `include` problem
`:2306` is about. **Nearly got wrong:** I almost went back to `include`. The tell that saved it was
the frame naming a PRODUCT file (`Failed to resolve import "@/…" from apps/ui/components/SignUpFlow.tsx`)
— a resolve error inside a product file is a runner-config defect, `No test files found` is a filter
defect. Classify by cause first (COMMON §10.17) and this is a 30-second fix. Cost: ~2 min.

**3.3 "The sixteen consent files" was not a resolvable name.** The tree has **14** files matching
`consent-*`, totalling 159 tests. The packet's pinned figure `1 failed | 178 passed (179)` only
reproduces when `tests/render/auth-flow-integration.test.tsx` (18) and
`tests/unit/v2ui-node-runner.test.ts` (2) are added — 16 files, 179 tests. I reconciled it by
arithmetic in about three minutes; a seat that trusted the name would have reported 159 and been
"wrong" against a correct pin. **UPGRADE 3: a packet never names a FILE SET in prose. It gives the
list, or the command that produces it.** This is COMMON §10.10's corollary ("every COUNT carries its
counting rule and the command that produced it") applied to sets — the same rule, one level up, and
it is not currently stated for sets.

**3.4 Reading budget.** Getting to the first edit took the packet (30 lines), COMMON (143),
INSTRUCTIONS (99), BASELINE (45), four PLAN slices (~450), two review sections (~250), five ticket
bodies and one 8-comment thread. That is right — every one of them changed what I did — but the
**ordering** was luck. The single highest-value read was the packet's third "Orchestrator addition"
(the MEASURED TRUTH after the merges): it told me `run_c9`'s two arms cannot pass, which stopped me
from debugging a correct command for however long that would have taken. **UPGRADE 4: a packet's
"what is already known to be broken, and what you do instead" block belongs at the TOP, above the
reading list, beside CLAIM.** Right now it is the last section of the file.

